gZo(){return"This digit isn't the right one for this cell. Erase it."},
gZq(){return"This digit is one too many: it repeats in its row, column or box (tinted cells). Erase it."},
gZp(){return"This digit is wrong: because of it, the tinted cell has no possible digit left. Erase it."},
gZr(){return"This cell's notes no longer contain the right digit: one was struck out by mistake. Redo them by looking at its row, column and box."},
Zl(a){var s=""+a
return"Only "+s+" is still possible in this cell: every other digit is already ruled out, by the tinted cells or by your notes. Place the "+s+"."},
Zj(a,b){var s=""+a
return"In "+b+", the "+s+" can only go here: the other cells are blocked by a "+s+" already placed (tinted cells) or by your notes. Place the "+s+"."},
gZi(){return"What comes next needs reasoning on notes, and some cells had none: I wrote every digit still possible in the empty cells for you (you can undo). Ask for another hint for the deduction."},
Zn(a,b){var s=""+a
return"In this box, the "+s+" can only go on "+b+" (tinted cells). So it will be in the box, on that line: strike the "+s+" from the notes of the outlined cells, on the rest of "+b+"."},
Zf(a,b){var s=""+a
return"In "+b+", the "+s+" can only go in one box (tinted cells). So it is there: strike the "+s+" from the notes of the other outlined cells of that box."},
Zm(a,b){return"In "+b+", the tinted cells can only hold the digits "+a+": those digits are reserved for them. Strike them from the notes of the other cells of "+b+" (outlined cells)."},
Zk(a,b){return"In "+b+", the digits "+a+" only have room in the outlined cells: they are reserved for them. Strike every other digit from their notes."},
Ld(a,b,c){var s=""+a
return"X-Wing on the "+s+": in each of these two "+b+", the "+s+" has only two places, in the same two "+c+" (tinted cells). It will take two opposite corners of that rectangle: strike the "+s+" from the notes of the outlined cells, in those "+c+"."},
Zg(a){var s=""+a
return"Try it: if this cell held "+s+", chaining simple deductions would lead to the tinted cell, left with no possible digit. So it isn't a "+s+": strike it from its notes."},
Zh(a){return"No classic technique applies to this grid right now. This cell's digit is "+a+"."},
alR(a){var s=null
return A.bM(a,s,this.a,s,"1 mine",""+a+" mines",s,s)},
galD(){return"Start anywhere: the first cell you reveal is never a mine, and it opens up something to reason on."},
galN(){return"This flag is misplaced: this cell isn't a mine. Take it off."},
alO(a){return"The tinted "+a+" touches more flags than its number: one of them is one too many, and it's this one. Take it off."},
alK(a){var s=""+a
return"The tinted "+s+" touches exactly "+s+" mines, and they are all flagged already. Its other neighbors (outlined) are therefore safe: reveal them."},
alJ(a,b){return"The tinted "+a+" still looks for "+b+", and it has only that many hidden cells around it (outlined): each one is a mine. Flag them."},
alM(a,b,c,d){var s=""+a,r=""+b
return"Look at the tinted "+s+" and "+r+". The "+s+" has only "+d+" hidden cells outside the ones it shares with the "+r+": it must put at least "+c+" in the shared cells. But the "+r+" only looks for "+c+" in total. Its other cells (outlined) have no room for a mine: reveal them."},
alL(a,b,c,d){var s=""+a,r=""+b
return"Look at the tinted "+s+" and "+r+". The "+s+" can't put more than "+c+" in the cells it shares with the "+r+". But the "+r+" looks for "+d+": it needs everything else in its other cells (outlined), and it has no more than that. They are all mines: flag them."},
galI(){return"Every mine is flagged already: the hidden cells left (outlined) are safe. Reveal them."},
galH(){return"There are as many hidden cells as mines left to find: they are all mines. Flag them."},
galF(){return"Simple rules aren't enough here. Trying every way of placing mines that fits the tinted numbers, this cell is never a mine: reveal it."},
galE(){return"Simple rules aren't enough here. Trying every way of placing mines that fits the tinted numbers, this cell is always a mine: flag it."},
alG(a){return"No cell is certainly safe. The least risky one is this: about a "+a+"% chance of being a mine. You can try it, or look elsewhere."},
